cheapest_falsifying_test:
1. **Bench test (2 weeks):** TGA or fixed-bed measurement of overnight (10-12 h) uptake at 420 ppm, 10-25 C and RH 20-60%, for amine-silica sheets and K2CO3-on-carbon sheets. Drop a variant if it stays below 0.5 mmol/g.
2. **Outdoor panels (~$15-30k, 2-3 months):** two 1 m2 panels outdoors in a dry sunny site, or under a solar simulator, run for 30 days with inlet/outlet CO2 analysers, a condenser and a gas meter.

The idea is falsified if any of these hold:
- sustained uptake below 0.15 kg CO2/m2/day;
- product purity below 90% after the air purge;
- capacity loss above 20% over 30 cycles. K2CO3 deliquesces above ~43% RH, so this is a known risk.

mechanism:
Clinker producers and importers face a declining cap on unabated process CO2 per t clinker: an illustrative −30% by 2035 and −90% by 2050, tradeable within the sector and border-adjusted for imports. Fuel CO2 stays under existing carbon pricing. Compliance counts only:
(a) CO2 stored in dedicated geological or mineral storage with measured injection (EOR excluded);
(b) verified clinker-ratio reduction;
(c) direct-separation process CO2 that is stored.
There is no public capex subsidy, because the cost passes to buildings at under 1%. Storage licences give Tier-1 queue priority to process CO2 and removals and leave fossil-power CCS last, which answers CAN's lock-in objection. Activist red lines met: CAN's 2021 objection to industrial CCS is partly evidential ('no definitive evidence'). This design removes the public-subsidy, EOR and fossil-power-life-extension elements CAN objects to on principle. Grounding precedent: Kigali, and the US 1990 Acid Rain Program, where regulation-driven end-of-pipe scrubbers scaled fast at a cost below forecasts (the FGD analogue Kazlou et al. 2024 use as the optimistic CCS growth case).
